-- title:
--   Theorem 4.2 — the SVT iterates converge to the unique solution of $\min \tau\|X\|_*+\tfrac12\|X\|_F^2$ s.t. $P_\Omega(X)=P_\Omega(M)$
-- statement:
--   Let $\tau>0$, let $\Omega\subseteq\{1,\dots,n_1\}\times\{1,\dots,n_2\}$ be a set of observed indices, and let $M\in\mathbb R^{n_1\times n_2}$. Suppose the step sizes obey
--   $$0<\inf_k\delta_k\le\sup_k\delta_k<2 .$$
--   Let $(X^k,Y^k)$ be generated by the singular value thresholding iteration (2.7) from $Y^0=0$:
--   $$X^k=\mathcal D_\tau(Y^{k-1}),\qquad Y^k=Y^{k-1}+\delta_kP_\Omega(M-X^k),\qquad k=1,2,\dots,$$
--   where $\mathcal D_\tau$ is the singular value shrinkage operator. Then the problem
--   $$\text{(2.8)}\qquad \text{minimize } \tau\|X\|_*+\tfrac12\|X\|_F^2\quad\text{subject to } P_\Omega(X)=P_\Omega(M)$$
--   has exactly one solution $X^\star$, and
--   $$\lim_{k\to\infty}X^k=X^\star .$$
--
--   This is the convergence guarantee of the SVT algorithm for matrix completion: for large $\tau$, the solution of (2.8) approximates the minimum nuclear norm completion of $M$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The step-size condition is written as: there are $a>0$ and $C<2$ with $a\le\delta_k\le C$ for all $k\ge1$ (explicit bounds instead of $\inf/\sup$, which Lean would evaluate to $0$ on an unbounded sequence). The start $Y^0=0$ is part of the hypothesis: the paper fixes it in (1.5), §2.4 and §5.1.3, and with a start that is nonzero outside $\Omega$ the iterates converge to a different matrix. Convergence is in the (Frobenius) topology of $\mathbb R^{n_1\times n_2}$; both uniqueness of the solution and convergence of the whole sequence to it are asserted.
-- source:
--   Cai, Candès, Shen, A Singular Value Thresholding Algorithm for Matrix Completion, SIAM J. Optim. 20 (2010), p. 1968, Theorem 4.2, second sentence; problem (2.8) p. 1961; iteration (2.7) p. 1961 with Y⁰ = 0 from (1.5) p. 1958

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CaiCandesShen_Convergence_Iterations
open Filter Topology

namespace CaiCandesShen.Convergence

/-- Theorem 4.2, second sentence, p. 1968: if `0 < inf δ_k ≤ sup δ_k < 2`, the sequence `X^k`
produced by the SVT iteration (2.7) from `Y⁰ = 0` converges to the unique solution of (2.8). -/
theorem svt_converges {n₁ n₂ : ℕ} (τ : ℝ) (hτ : 0 < τ) (Ω : Finset (Fin n₁ × Fin n₂))
    (M : Mat n₁ n₂) (δ : ℕ → ℝ)
    (hδ : ∃ a C : ℝ, 0 < a ∧ C < 2 ∧ ∀ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k → a ≤ δ k ∧ δ k ≤ C)
    (X Y : ℕ → Mat n₁ n₂) (hXY : IsSVTSeq τ Ω M δ X Y) :
    (∃! Xs : Mat n₁ n₂, IsSol28 τ Ω M Xs) ∧
      ∀ Xs : Mat n₁ n₂, IsSol28 τ Ω M Xs → Tendsto X atTop (𝓝 Xs) := by sorry

end CaiCandesShen.Convergence
